---
import WhoSignsIn from "../../components/graph/WhoSignsIn.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO } from "../../i18n/ui";
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="identity"
  eyebrow="Two tenants, two audiences"
  title="Identity and access"
  description="Parents use Entra External ID, administrators use a workforce app role, and each house authenticates to the API with its own device key."
>
  <p class="lede">
    The API accepts three credential types. Parents present Entra External ID tokens, administrators
    present workforce tokens with the <code>Lanternina.Admin</code> app role, and each house presents
    a device key over TLS. Each credential reaches a separate route group.
  </p>

  <WhoSignsIn
    title="Who signs in, and what they reach"
    desc="Three callers reach one API and three route groups. An Entra External ID token resolves a parent's household. A workforce token with the Lanternina.Admin app role authorizes account administration. A per-household device key authorizes work polling, approved-item retrieval and returned readings."
    caption="The API selects an authentication path from the route group and validates the credential before application logic runs."
    parent={{
      title: "A parent",
      sub: ["Entra External ID (CIAM)", "self-service registration"],
    }}
    admin={{
      title: "An administrator",
      sub: ["workforce tenant, app role", "Lanternina.Admin"],
    }}
    house={{
      title: "The house",
      sub: ["a per-household device key", "over TLS — no Entra identity"],
    }}
    api={{
      title: "One API",
      sub: ["separate audiences, checked", "before any logic of ours runs"],
    }}
    parentRoutes={{ title: "Parent routes", sub: ["their own household only"] }}
    adminRoutes={{ title: "Admin routes", sub: ["admitting and rejecting accounts"] }}
    deviceRoutes={{
      title: "Device routes",
      sub: ["ask for work, fetch approved", "items, return a reading"],
    }}
  />

  <h2>Separate identities for parents and administrators</h2>

  <p>
    Entra External ID supports self-service parent registration. Administrative privilege comes
    from a separate workforce app registration, rather than a flag in the Cosmos container that an
    administrator edits. The privilege and the application data therefore have separate control
    planes and bootstrap paths.
  </p>

  <p>
    The administrator's token carries the privilege in its <code>roles</code> claim. Parent and admin
    applications have different audiences, and the API checks the audience before application
    logic. Only a workforce token reaches the app-role check on administrative routes.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    An app-role assignment can land on <b>Default Access</b>, whose <code>appRoleId</code> is all zeros.
    The resulting token is valid but has no <code>roles</code> claim. Inspect
    <code>servicePrincipals/&lt;id&gt;/appRoleAssignedTo</code> and verify the assigned id when an admin
    token passes authentication but fails authorization.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-tenant by construction</h2>

  <p>
    Every stored row is partitioned by household id. Each device key maps to one household, and each
    parent route resolves the household from the validated token. Adding a house requires a
    provisioning run and uses the existing schema.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a claim about shape, and it is worth being precise about how far it has been exercised:
    the code has always run with one household in it. A second house has never been provisioned, so
    "multi-tenant" here means the boundaries are drawn and enforced, not that two tenants have been
    observed side by side.
  </p>

  <h2>House authentication</h2>

  <p>
    The hub presents its device key in the <code>X-Device-Key</code> header over TLS. The API holds
    the Azure credential and performs model calls for page reading, reminder interpretation and
    activity continuation. Device routes return 503 until a device key is configured.
  </p>

  <p>
    A hub therefore carries a credential for its household routes rather than a credential that can
    be replayed against the Azure subscription. This boundary makes cloud connectivity a requirement
    for page reading.
  </p>

  <h2>Separate approval and safety keys</h2>

  <p>
    Content safety and parental approval use separate HMAC keys. The approval seal covers the safety
    seal, so the content-safety component can screen content but cannot mint a parent's decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    The approval seal is minted and verified within one device execution, so its key can rotate
    between runs. The safety seal crosses processes and days: the gate mints it, the panel stores it,
    and a later process verifies it. The hub filesystem was searched for both literal key values to
    verify that neither key is deployed there.
  </p>

  <h2>OIDC deployment</h2>

  <p>
    GitHub Actions exchanges its OIDC token for an Azure token through a federated credential on a
    user-assigned managed identity. Three resource-scoped role assignments allow the pipeline to
    push to one registry, read that registry and update one container app.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    The subject of a federated credential carries numeric ids, not names:
    <code>repo:owner@12345/repo@67890:ref:refs/heads/main</code>. The rejection message quotes the
    subject it received in full, so the reliable method is to register once, run once, and read the
    real subject out of the refusal rather than writing it from memory. It also survives a rename of
    the repository.
  </p>

  <h2>Known authorization gaps</h2>

  <p>
    The token validator checks audience, issuer, expiry and subject but omits <code>scp</code>. The
    current app exposes one scope. Adding another scope would require a scope check before it could
    separate permissions.
  </p>

  <p>
    App registrations are provisioned manually from the documented steps. The manifest must set
    <code>requestedAccessTokenVersion: 2</code>; the default v1 issuer does not match the API's token
    validation configuration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/DEPLOY.md`}>The deployment steps, including the manual ones</a> ·
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/WORKLOAD-IDENTITY.md`}
      >how the pipeline changes Azure with no secret anywhere</a
    > · <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`}>what leaves the device</a>
  </p>
</Base>
